## 7. Architecture and engineering rules

### 7.1 Technology baseline

- C++20.
- JUCE 9.0.4 in the current working environment.
- CMake.
- Visual Studio 2026 / MSVC x64.
- VST3 effect and Standalone targets.
- Native JUCE Components.
- Existing Inter font assets and FontManager.

The original Revision 2 references to JUCE 8, Visual Studio 2022 and VS2022 CMake presets are historical and must not be copied into new implementation instructions unless the project is deliberately migrated.

### 7.2 Architectural boundaries

Target separation:

- `core/`: models, algorithms, service interfaces and thread-safe data structures. No GUI dependency.
- `plugin/`: JUCE AudioProcessor, plugin state and host integration.
- `ui/`: components, theme, layout, navigation and interaction.
- `services/`: library, preview, waveform, analysis, assistant and drag services, implemented behind clear interfaces.
- `tests/`: unit and integration tests.
- `assets/`: fonts, icons, reference art and fixtures.
- `docs/`: specifications, test records and developer instructions.

The UI must not query SQLite directly or perform disk scans. It should consume immutable results supplied by service interfaces.

### 7.3 Threading contract

This contract is mandatory.

| Thread | Allowed | Forbidden |
|---|---|---|
| Audio callback (`processBlock`) | Read atomics, use preallocated buffers, push/pop lock-free queues, mix prepared preview audio | Allocation, mutexes, file/network I/O, logging, blocking waits, GUI calls |
| Message/UI thread | Paint components, process user input, apply completed worker results | Blocking I/O, audio decoding, database queries, network waits |
| Worker threads | File scanning, audio decoding, waveform generation, database queries and analysis | Directly accessing or mutating UI components |
| Network thread | HTTPS requests and streaming responses to/from backend | Any work on the audio callback |

Communication mechanisms may include atomics, lock-free FIFOs, `juce::AbstractFifo`, `AsyncUpdater` and safe message-thread dispatch. Worker results must be marshalled to the UI thread before updating components.

The audio thread must not wait for a worker, lock a mutex or allocate memory. Preview data must be decoded and prepared off-thread, then transferred using a real-time-safe handoff.

### 7.4 Plugin state

Use versioned state serialization. Persist only data that belongs in plugin state, such as:
- User parameter values.
- Selected workspace and relevant UI state.
- Assistant panel open/closed state.
- User-approved plugin preferences.

Do not store provider API secrets in plugin state, presets or project files. Large library databases and waveform caches belong in the application data directory, not inside the DAW project or VST3 bundle.

### 7.5 Plugin identity

Keep the current codes stable:
- Plugin ID/code: `AmE1`
- Manufacturer code: `RnMu`

Do not change these values without a deliberate migration/release decision.

