## 14. Dependencies, licensing, privacy and release obligations

### 14.1 JUCE

The project currently uses JUCE 9.0.4. JUCE is dual-licensed; commercial closed-source distribution requires confirming and obtaining the appropriate licence. Verify current terms directly with JUCE before release.

### 14.2 Fonts and visual assets

Inter font files and the corresponding licence are included in the repository. Preserve the licence and verify that all additional fonts, icons, artwork and sample fixtures may be redistributed in the intended product.

### 14.3 Audio analysis and DSP dependencies

Do not add a third-party library until:
- Its licence has been reviewed for the intended commercial distribution.
- Its binary redistribution terms are understood.
- Its platform and compiler compatibility are confirmed.
- Its CPU and memory impact are tested.

GPL/AGPL dependencies require particular care for a closed-source commercial plugin.

### 14.4 Sample rights

For local user-owned files, preserve the user's original file and metadata. For online or generated content, track source and licence/usage restrictions. Do not imply that a sample is royalty-free or commercially cleared unless the source licence supports that claim.

### 14.5 Privacy

Any audio upload, account telemetry or cloud processing requires clear disclosure and appropriate user consent. Keep credentials out of logs and project files. Define retention and deletion policies before backend release.

### 14.6 Distribution

Before public distribution:
- Confirm JUCE and all dependency licences.
- Prepare third-party notices.
- Consider code signing.
- Build a reliable installer/uninstaller.
- Test on a clean Windows machine.
- Document supported Windows and DAW versions.
- Archive release symbols and exact build information.

